{"ocrText": "Book Reviews\nThe Twelfth Planet-\nZechafia Sitchin\n384pp. Illustrated &\nreferenced.\nGeorge Allen & Unwia f5.50.\n' Not another Ancient Aitronauts book !'\nwas my immediate reacdon on re-\nceiving a copy of The Twelfth Planet.\nWell-it is and it isn't! Certainly\nSitchin is to be congratulated on the\ndiligence of his research, for he parts\ncompany with many other authors\ninsamuch as nourhere does one find the\n(often dubious) quotes from books and\narticles with a similar theme. Going\nback to 'source'-the original Sum-\nerian-Sitchin demonstrates, with over\n160 illustrations, how there would\nseem to be overwhelming evidence for\na 'twelfth planet '-yes, twelfth and\nnot tenth as one would think-with an\neccentric orbit of some 3600 years.\nHe then puts forward the view that\nthe hypothetical inhabitants thereof-\nthe Nefilim-have an important part\nin human affairs, even to the extent of\n'creating Man in their own image'\nby gene manipulation.\nWhilst having reservations about the\napparent longevity of the Nefilim, and\nhow their planet manages to retain\nsufficient heat and light to support\n'human' life whilst presumably or-\nbiting through realms many times the\ndistance of Pluto, one cannot help\nfeeling that Sitchin makes his point-\nand makes it well. My two com-\nplaints are firstly, that the book stops\nshort of the New Testament era-a\ntime when the hypothetical planet\nwould again have been in the Sun's\nneighbourhood, and secondly, that if\nthe planet exists, it is now in the outer-\nmost reaches of its orbit and the\n6\npossibility of practical confirmation\nwould therefore seem to approach zero.\nSo far as the first point goes, however,\nI understand there is a possibility of\nSitchin producing a sequCl.\nThe argument-albeit at times one\nrather balks at the volume of Middle-\nEastern historical and' pre-historical'\nreferences and successions-is ex-\ntremely well put. An intriguing book\n-READ IT!\nNorman Olioq\n\" UFOs Explained \"\nPhilip f Klass\nPublished by Vintage Booksr1970\n$2.{5 (about f,l;90).\nGenuinely sceptical books about UFOs\nare few and far between. This book\nis undoubtedly one, wrinen by an\nauthor who, along with the late Donald\nMenzel, has been one of the most\nconsistent' anti-UFO' propagandists.\nPhilip Klass is an American ttchnical\niournalist who gained some prom-\ninence in 1966 with his publicizing of\nthe plasma theory of UFO's. This\nwas expounded at greater length in his\nfirst book \" UFO's ldentified.\" The\ntheoryis given less emphasis in this, his\nlatest book, which is essentially a long\npolernic against the extra-terrestrial\nhypothesis for UFO's.\nMany familiar cases are dealt with,\nranging from the 1897' airship' sight-\nings to the 1973174 US flap. There", "textLayer": [{"text": "Book", "x": 7.72, "y": 5.31, "w": 7.19, "h": 2.74, "fontSize": 14.5}, {"text": "Reviews", "x": 14.98, "y": 5.56, "w": 9.52, "h": 2.45, "fontSize": 13.0}, {"text": "The", "x": 7.77, "y": 8.95, "w": 4.46, "h": 2.17, "fontSize": 11.5}, {"text": "Twelfth", "x": 12.38, "y": 8.87, "w": 8.15, "h": 2.26, "fontSize": 12.0}, {"text": "Planet-", "x": 20.61, "y": 8.83, "w": 7.68, "h": 2.36, "fontSize": 12.5}, {"text": "Zechafia Sitchin", "x": 7.86, "y": 10.8, "w": 15.47, "h": 2.11, "fontSize": 11.0}, {"text": "384pp.", "x": 7.68, "y": 12.93, "w": 7.44, "h": 2.17, "fontSize": 11.5}, {"text": "Illustrated", "x": 15.4, "y": 12.86, "w": 10.73, "h": 2.26, "fontSize": 12.0}, {"text": "&", "x": 26.24, "y": 13.07, "w": 1.42, "h": 2.07, "fontSize": 11.0}, {"text": "referenced.", "x": 7.72, "y": 14.5, "w": 9.81, "h": 1.89, "fontSize": 10.0}, {"text": "George", "x": 7.77, "y": 16.71, "w": 7.01, "h": 1.89, "fontSize": 10.0}, {"text": "Allen", "x": 14.84, "y": 16.56, "w": 5.33, "h": 2.07, "fontSize": 11.0}, {"text": "&", "x": 20.43, "y": 16.67, "w": 1.92, "h": 1.98, "fontSize": 10.5}, {"text": "Unwia", "x": 22.48, "y": 16.6, "w": 6.52, "h": 2.07, "fontSize": 11.0}, {"text": "f5.50.", "x": 29.27, 